{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794514688,"nonce":"qfpJV3h+S5dwOiH4wg2hhMQPg+U=","title":"A very long list of children blocks the first frame: building it runs before anything is drawn","message":"scroll_bench with LIST=flat and 100000 rows shows the window at 0.8 s and nothing in it until 17.9 s; with LIST=virtual (virtual_list) the rows are there in about 2 s of which most is process start. In the harness (release-debug, 100000 rows) the UI build and layout before any frame take about 12 s, down from 29 s once TextShaper kept the rustybuzz face and shape plans (c39fe944). The first paint after that is about 150 ms. Rendering should not wait on data of any size.\n\nWhat the 12 s is has not been profiled since the shaping fix. Before it, nearly all of it was text measurement during layout (200k text nodes), then the building of 300k nodes.\n\nDirections, not decided:\n- The idiom is virtual_list, which builds only the rows in view; say so where a scroll container is given thousands of children, and keep scroll_bench's LIST=flat as the stress case.\n- Build a long child list over frames: mount the first screen, draw, and add the rest in chunks, so the window is never blank. The scroll container's content size would need the rows not yet added.\n- Reduce the per-node build and measure cost: profile the build at 100k and look at what is left.\n\nScroll rows work (3b364ef) made a repaint of a long list cost what is in view, not the list; the build is now the O(rows) cost left on this path.","files":null}]}